cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or benchmark, please cite it as below. Research prototype, not for clinical use."
title: "VetAgentBench-IN: do LLM agents give safer veterinary advice than a bare LLM?"
type: software
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-10-19"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Shubhomoy919/VetAgent"
authors:
  - family-names: Sarkar
    given-names: Shubhomoy
abstract: >-
  A benchmark and research prototype that runs 29 India-focused, livestock-heavy veterinary cases through five
  conditions (bare LLM, retrieval, tools, self-verification, deterministic guardrails) with one answer schema,
  measuring dangerous errors (false-safe and illegal drugs, under-triage, invented withdrawal periods, missed
  escalation) and accuracy, with Wilson intervals and McNemar tests. Knowledge source: India's Standard Veterinary
  Treatment Guidelines (DAHD, 2024). Benchmark v1.0.0 content hash
  b4b15a6930708f0976cb2b119f389c6d32ac321f8e2715d1751fed7c721edf07. Gold answers are not yet veterinarian-verified.
keywords:
  - veterinary
  - large language models
  - agents
  - benchmark
  - India
  - patient safety
